{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what happens to unprotected gutters at a <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> vacation home:</p>

<p>You leave in October, gutters are clear. November drops leaves. December drops more, plus pine needles. January rains compact everything into a soggy mass. February and March, that mass holds water against the gutter bottom while humidity keeps it wet. By April, you've got standing water, corrosion starting, and debris breaking down into muck.</p>

<p>You come back in May. The gutters look okay from the ground. But water isn't flowing right. And behind one section, the fascia is soft and stained — moisture has been wicking into the wood for months.</p>

<p>I've seen this exact scenario dozens of times in Jackson County. The homeowner had no idea anything was wrong until the gutter pulled away from rotted wood. At that point, you're not just cleaning gutters — you're replacing fascia, maybe soffit, maybe more.</p>

<p>Gutter guards prevent this cycle by keeping debris out in the first place. Nothing to accumulate, nothing to hold moisture, nothing to cause damage while you're away.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">full-time Scottsboro residents</a>, guards are a convenience — you stop climbing ladders and paying for cleanings. For vacation homeowners, they're risk management. Different stakes.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are covers or screens that let water into your gutters while blocking debris. Simple concept, but execution varies wildly by product type.</p>

<p><strong>Foam inserts:</strong> The cheap stuff at Home Depot. Water flows through porous foam, debris sits on top. Problem: the foam absorbs humidity and stays wet. Near Lake Guntersville, they're saturated most of the time. Mold grows in them. They break down into chunks within 2-3 years. Don't buy them.</p>

<p><strong>Brush guards:</strong> Bristles that fill the gutter. Same problem — they trap fine debris between bristles, hold moisture, and create a mess that's harder to clean than an unprotected gutter. Skip.</p>

<p><strong>Screen guards:</strong> Perforated covers with larger openings. Better than foam, but pine needles and seed pods go right through the holes. If you've got pine trees, screens aren't enough.</p>

<p><strong>Solid surface tension covers:</strong> Water sheets over a curved surface, debris slides off. Works well in moderate conditions but can get overwhelmed in heavy rain — and North Alabama storms are rarely moderate.</p>

<p><strong>Micro-mesh guards:</strong> Fine stainless steel mesh over a solid aluminum frame. Blocks even pine needles (holes are typically 50-150 microns — smaller than a needle tip), handles heavy rain flow, and doesn't absorb moisture. This is what we install on most Scottsboro properties.</p>

<p>Installation means attaching the guards securely to the gutter edge and sliding them under the first course of shingles — no penetrations through your roofing. Takes 2-4 hours for a typical home.</p>
{% endset %}

